imud NEWS — user-visible changes
================================

Newest first. This file records notable, user-visible changes per release. The
per-package changelogs under /usr/share/doc/<pkg>/ hold the packaging-level
record (changelog.Debian.gz in a .deb, changelog.gz from a source install), and
the git history holds the full detail.

Entries from 1.7 onward are generated from docs/release-notes.toml; edit the
registry rather than this file.


1.10.0
------
Drivers can now expose their part's built-in self-test, and imud-imutest
grades it — the first passive check that proves the sensing elements are live.

Added:

* imud-imutest runs the IMU's built-in self-test and grades the per-axis
  response against the datasheet window. It needs no motion and no operator,
  so it runs under --passive.
  This is the only passive check that distinguishes a live sensing element
  from a dead one: at rest both read about zero and pass every other check.
  Implemented for ism330dhcx, and for mpu9250/mpu9255/mpu6500 where the
  response is recorded rather than graded because TDK publishes no window for
  it. Other drivers structurally skip it and stay clearable.
* New driver mpu6500 for the TDK MPU-6500, the six-axis part inside the
  MPU-925x. Boards sold as MPU-9250 are frequently relabelled MPU-6500s; those
  now run as the six-axis IMU they are, with a separate magnetometer in [mag]
  for heading.
  The mpu9250 and mpu9255 probes already refused such a board; they now name
  mpu6500 as the driver to use.
* imud builds and runs on big-endian hosts. The binary packet and the .imucap
  file are converted field by field instead of memcpy'd in host order, so they
  stay little-endian whatever the machine, and the Debian packages are now
  Architecture: linux-any.
  No wire-format change of its own: the bytes a little-endian host emits are
  identical to before. The packet does move to v18 in this release, for a
  separate change.
* A gyro+accelerometer board with no magnetometer runs, with [mag] driver =
  "none". imud opens no magnetometer bus and claims no [mag] int_gpio; roll,
  pitch, heave, the sea-state statistics and rate of turn are unaffected.
  Heading starts at zero and is relative to the orientation imud booted in,
  not to earth north, and drifts at the gyro's bias-instability rate.
  FLAG_MAG_VALID stays clear for the whole run and no declination is applied.
* New imud-influxdb key detail chooses how much of the packet to write, as
  five cumulative levels: attitude, navigation, seastate, health, full. full
  adds the calibrated and raw accel, gyro and mag vectors, which the bridge
  never published.
  The default, health, is exactly what the bridge wrote before, so no existing
  series or dashboard changes. full is 59 fields against health's 38.

Changed:

* mpu6500 is no longer experimental. It is validated on real silicon by an
  imud-imutest --all report: 61 checks passed, none failed. The daemon no
  longer warns at startup when it is selected.
* Wire format v18: the packet grows 276 to 288 bytes with flags_ext, a second
  32-bit flag word, and eight reserved bytes. Every existing field keeps its
  offset. Its first bit says no magnetometer is configured.
  Breaking: consumers reject a wire version they do not know, so deploy the
  daemon and its consumers together. flags had assigned all sixteen bits,
  which is what forced the new word — a consumer must ignore bits it does not
  recognise, and a later flag then needs no further bump. The imud-arduino
  client needs its own update.
* The bridges say when a heading is not referenced to north, each in its
  protocol's own terms rather than by all withholding it.
  imud-signalk drops navigation.headingMagnetic/True/magneticVariation; imud-
  mqtt drops the matching topics, which are retained, and adds
  navigation/headingReferenced. imud-influxdb keeps publishing heading with
  heading_ref and mag_absent fields, and imud-prometheus keeps its gauge and
  adds imud_mag_uncal and imud_mag_absent. imud-mavlink gains SYS_STATUS,
  whose 3D_MAG present and health bits separate a board with no magnetometer
  from one that has stopped answering.
* NMEA withholds $HCHDM, $HCHDG and $HCHDT, and leaves $PASHR's heading field
  null, while no magnetometer is being fused. An uncalibrated magnetometer
  still publishes all of them.
  A rig with no magnetometer, or one that has stopped delivering, used to
  publish a dead-reckoned heading as magnetic heading; no heading sentence has
  a field that could qualify it. Consumers now get no heading rather than a
  wrong one. $TIROT and $IIXDR are unaffected, and imud-mon shows heading as
  --.

Fixed:

* imud-imutest's guided gyro and spin phases integrated every angle 1000x low.
  A commanded 90 degree turn reported 0.1 degrees, and the tool blamed the
  driver's FIFO for it.
  Affects 1.9.0 and 1.9.1. Reports from those versions show gyro.*.sign,
  gyro.*.scale, imu.direct.gyro and spin.frame_agreement failing or skipping
  on hardware that was working correctly; re-run to grade the part.
* imud-imutest now shows 'Enter when done' while a guided turn is collecting.
  The hint only appeared when there was nothing else on the line, so operators
  let every turn run to its timeout.
* The packet's fifo_overflow flag (bit 7) is now actually set when samples are
  lost, and the daemon logs the gap at 1 Hz saying whether the hardware FIFO
  overflowed or the internal ring dropped.
  The bit has been documented since the format was first published and was
  never raised, so no consumer could detect a sample gap; only imud-status's
  counter showed one, and not when it happened. It is latched for about two
  seconds so a slow consumer still sees it.
* mpu925x reset() now waits the MPU-925x product spec's register start-up time
  before reading the part back, and resets the gyro, accel and temperature
  signal paths as TDK's own Linux driver does for this generation.
  The previous settle was the accelerometer's cold-start figure, which is
  shorter than the interval before a register access is guaranteed to answer
  at all.
* The armhf packages are now built on Raspbian at the ARMv6 baseline, so they
  run on every Raspberry Pi that boots a 32-bit userland. Previously they were
  built to Debian's ARMv7 baseline.
  On a Pi 1 or Pi Zero/Zero W the previous packages installed without
  complaint and then died on an illegal instruction, because both ports report
  the same "armhf" architecture. Those boards can now install from the apt
  repository. The arm64 packages are unchanged.


1.9.1
-----
A correctness release. The magnetometer is now used for heading even without a
calibration, which replaces an unbounded heading drift with a bounded one.

Changed:

* An uncalibrated magnetometer is now fused for heading instead of ignored.
  Heading is offset by the uncorrected hard iron but bounded, where before it
  dead-reckoned without bound. Heading-only, so roll and pitch are untouched,
  and withdrawn automatically under gross iron.
  New flag bit 15, FLAG_MAG_UNCAL, marks the uncalibrated-but-fused state.
  FLAG_MAG_VALID is unchanged and still means calibrated and healthy, so
  existing consumers need no change. The wire packet is byte-identical and
  still v17. This spends the last free flag bit. Set mag_fuse_uncal = false to
  restore the old behaviour.
  ACTION: Run imud-cal mag for a heading accurate enough to navigate on; this
  is a floor, not a substitute.

Fixed:

* The accelerometer update no longer corrects heading or shrinks the attitude
  covariance about the gravity axis. Gravity says nothing about rotation about
  gravity, and doing either could collapse the covariance and report the
  filter converged while heading was wrong.
  With no magnetometer aiding heading, cov[9] and FLAG_FUSION_CONVERGED now
  report an unaided heading axis honestly: the covariance grows and the
  converged flag stays clear. A consumer that treated the flag as permission
  to trust heading was previously misled in exactly that case.
* imud-imutest no longer recommends clearing experimental on a gyro that
  produced nothing: the per-axis sign check now requires a quarter of the
  commanded turn, so a dead data path fails instead of passing on noise.
  A report generated before this could pass all three gyro sign checks on
  integrated noise while the scale checks failed against the same value. Re-
  run imud-imutest for any driver cleared on such a report.


1.9.0
-----
An additive release: SPI transport, higher sample rates on three parts, a new
magnetometer driver, and the manual as an Info page. The wire packet is
unchanged — still v17 / 276 bytes — and so is the libimud ABI, so nothing here
forces you to redeploy consumers. Every new key defaults to the behaviour
1.8.2 had.

Added:

* SPI transport for the IMU and the magnetometer. [imu] and [mag] each gain
  bus, spi_dev and spi_speed_hz, and choose independently.
  Default is bus = "i2c"; existing configs are unaffected.
* New magnetometer driver rm3100 (PNI RM3100): magneto-inductive, no SET/RESET
  coil, I2C or SPI, 1-600 Hz. Experimental until validated on hardware.
* Higher sample rates: ism330dhcx and lsm6dso gain 3332 and 6664 Hz, icm42688p
  4000 through 32000 Hz. These are rates the datasheets always specified and
  the drivers had never offered.
  6664 Hz needs SPI; icm42688p above 8000 Hz will not run on a Raspberry Pi.
* The manual installs as an Info page: info imud.
  Building from source now requires texinfo.
* Sample latency is instrumented. The [stats] line splits it into FIFO
  residence, which your fifo_wm and rate decide, and the daemon's own
  pipeline.
* imud-status says when heading is dead-reckoned. Without a magnetometer
  calibration the filter's mag update never runs and heading drifts on the
  gyro alone, which nothing in the output used to show.
* Startup warns when [imu] spi_speed_hz is below 2.5 MHz and a magnetometer
  shares the same SPI controller, naming the rate to use — below that the
  magnetometer can stop measuring while still answering the bus.

Changed:

* Sample rates are carried in milli-Hz throughout, so the advertised rates are
  now the silicon's own. The ISM330DHCX ladder is 6664/2^n, making its bottom
  rung 13.016 Hz where the datasheet prints 12.5.
  odr_hz accepts fractional rates. A rate that rounds to zero is refused.
* imud --replay exits when the capture runs out, after letting the last
  samples reach the filter. It used to sit there until killed.
* Replaying one capture twice now produces identical filter output, and
  sim_speed decides the pace rather than the reader threads' hardware poll
  cadences.
* A reader thread that gives up stops the daemon instead of leaving it running
  with a dead sensor. A magnetometer that stops delivering is reported once
  per outage, and again when it recovers.
* Heave and sea state accumulate in double, so a long run no longer loses
  resolution in the integrators.

Fixed:

* Calibration was applied in the wrong frame when [mount] was not identity:
  the daemon rotated board to body first and applied the sensor-frame
  calibration second.
  Only affects a rotated mount; with [mount] identity the two frames coincide.
  ACTION: Re-run imud-cal for any sensor on a non-identity mount.
* The daemon could not start on arm64 or armhf under its own systemd unit, and
  installing or upgrading could leave a Raspberry Pi 5 unable to open its GPIO
  chip.
* Several sample-timestamp defects: chip_ts could run backwards between
  bursts, a corrupted read could stamp a whole burst seconds into the future,
  the timer period was trusted rather than measured, and timestamps carried
  two offsets.
* The magnetometer ran at a third of its configured rate: the driver confirmed
  each sample by reading Meas_M_Done, but that bit is also the interrupt
  acknowledge, so a reader blocked on the edge never saw it set.
  Only affected an installation with [mag] int_gpio set.
* imud-cal mag could write a soft-iron scale the swing never measured,
  stretching an axis to cover data that was never taken. An axis must now be
  swept through at least 0.8 of the fitted radius, capping any correction at
  1.25x.
* An MPU FIFO overflow desynchronised every later read: the bytes the part
  drops to make room are never a whole number of sample-sets, and this FIFO
  carries no tag to resync from.
* imud-imutest measures the magnetometer the way the daemon reads it, and
  counts data-ready edges against the drain rather than a timer of its own.
* imud-influxdb half-applied its output enables on SIGHUP, so a reload could
  leave some outputs on and others off.


1.8.2
-----
A correctness release: bad configuration and bad calibration are now refused
rather than absorbed.

Added:

* New flag bit FLAG_STATE_RESET (bit 14): the filter found a non-finite value
  in its own state, reset, and is re-aligning.

Changed:

* A bad value in a config file is fatal instead of silently accepted. An over-
  long path was truncated and used, an out-of-range integer wrapped, and nan,
  inf and 1e999 loaded cleanly into any float key.
* imud --config PATH means that file and no other; it no longer falls back to
  a different config when the named one is missing.

Fixed:

* A non-finite or absurdly large calibration poisoned the filter permanently.
  cal.json values are now validated on load.
* SIGHUP reload failed outright on a daemon started from a $HOME config, and
  deleting a key and reloading kept the old value.
* A failed output thread left its socket bound with nothing behind it, so a
  client connected successfully and then waited forever.
* The shipped systemd unit forbade the gpiochip the shipped config asked for.
* imud-imutest accepted garbage in its numeric options rather than rejecting
  it.


1.8.1
-----
A packaging and hardening release. The group change affects every
installation.

Added:

* All six units gain tier-2 systemd sandboxing, and every file descriptor the
  daemon, tools and client libraries open is close-on-exec.

Changed:

* Device access is governed by a new imud group rather than gpio. Every unit
  hardcoded Group=gpio, but nothing created it, so imud did not start on stock
  Debian.
  ACTION: Add any user who runs the tools by hand to the imud group.
* A configured rate of zero is a fatal config error rather than a silent
  fallback.

Fixed:

* The two bridge configs that hold credentials were world-readable. They are
  now installed 0640, owned by the imud group.
* One configured ODR now means one rate everywhere. The filter was tuned from
  a rate rounded differently than the one the driver programmed.
* A config file that exists but cannot be read was reported as "disabled in
  config".
* A client that connected to the metrics port and then said nothing could
  stall the exporter.


1.8
---
Hardware validation tooling, two new driver families, and a much smaller
source tarball.

Added:

* New tool imud-imutest: validates a sensor driver against real hardware and
  writes a Markdown report to attach to an issue. This is how an experimental
  driver becomes a supported one.
* New drivers mpu9250 and mpu9255 (IMU) with ak8963 (magnetometer), for the
  InvenSense/TDK MPU-925x 9-axis breakouts. All three experimental.

Changed:

* Release artifacts carry a verifiable provenance attestation, and the source
  tarball no longer contains binaries.

Fixed:

* imud-cal accel printed the wrong physical instructions for all six
  positions, so an operator following them produced a calibration for the
  wrong orientations.
* imud-cal mag over-stated the soft-iron correction on any board with hard
  iron.
* The IMU chip timer's declared period was trusted rather than measured, so a
  part whose oscillator ran off nominal produced timestamps that drifted.


1.7
---
A filter release. The wire packet grows to v17 (276 bytes) with four new
diagnostic fields, and the MEKF gains a wave-acceleration state that models
the correlated part of the gravity residual in a seaway.

Added:

* The MEKF error state grows from 6 to 9 with a Gauss-Markov wave-acceleration
  term. In a seaway the gravity residual is wave-orbital and time-correlated,
  which no single white noise value can describe.
* New imud-cal fit-ra mode: replays a rough-water capture through the filter
  and reports whether the noise model matches the water it was recorded in.
* New [fusion] key mekf_mag_dip_sigma_deg and new [mount] key rotation_matrix,
  which takes the board-to-body rotation directly as nine row-major elements.

Changed:

* Wire format v17: the packet grows 260 to 276 bytes with four MEKF diagnostic
  fields — two reporting how hard the update gates are working, two reporting
  whether the noise model is honest.
  Consumers reading the raw packet must be rebuilt and deployed with the
  daemon; libimud applications are unaffected.
* An unrecognised [mount] preset name is a fatal error rather than a warning
  that silently left the mount unrotated.
* Flag bit 6 (FLAG_MOTION) is formally retired. It was defined but never set.

Fixed:

* The initial attitude alignment averaged a hardcoded one second — about a
  fifth of a roll period — so a daemon started underway aligned to an
  arbitrary point in the wave cycle.
  The window is now align_window_sec, default 5 s.
* The MEKF declared its innovation covariance singular on an absolute
  determinant threshold, discarding most accelerometer updates in ordinary
  conditions. The test is now relative to the matrix scale.
* The gross-outlier reject gate was rejecting a quarter of ordinary wave
  motion, and the health metrics used a gain that assumed every sample
  produced an update, making their true time constant ten minutes instead of
  thirty seconds.
* A config reload no longer leaves the engine-vibration state half-applied,
  and it recomputes the magnetic reference adaptation rate when the
  magnetometer rate changes.
* imud-signalk's dest_addr accepted a numeric IPv4 address only, while every
  other bridge resolved a hostname.


1.6.2
-----
* Internal refactor: duplicated code across the daemon, drivers, and
  bridges consolidated into shared helpers — no behavior, wire, config,
  or log-output change. The five bridge daemons now share their startup
  and main-loop scaffolding (src/bridge.c + src/sdnotify.c), all I2C
  drivers share the register-access helpers (src/drivers/i2c_io.h), and
  the WMM declination/field-reference derivation is a single function
  used by both the static-position and GPS paths. Net −835 lines.
* New test suite (test_bridge) covers the shared bridge scaffolding:
  emit-tick timing math, CLI handling, config load/reload, systemd
  notification delivery, and stream reconnect behavior.


1.6.1
-----
* Bridge output posture — daemon on, outputs off. Every bridge
  (imud-signalk, imud-mqtt, imud-influxdb, imud-mavlink, imud-prometheus)
  now separates the daemon-level `enabled` from its per-output enables. A
  stock installed config enables the daemon but ships every output OFF, so
  the service starts, stays healthy under systemd, and reads imud's stream
  while emitting nothing until you turn on the output(s) you want. This
  matches how the main imud daemon already treats its section enables.
  - imud-signalk: new `udp_enabled` gates the UDP delta output, independent
    of the existing `tcp_enabled` (enable either or both). BEHAVIOR CHANGE:
    UDP used to send whenever the daemon ran; an existing config that relied
    on that must now add `udp_enabled = true`.
  - imud-influxdb: new `udp_enabled` and `http_enabled` (both may run at
    once) replace the `transport = "udp"|"http"` selector. `transport` is
    DEPRECATED but still honored — when neither enable is set it is mapped
    to the matching output with a one-time warning, so existing configs keep
    working.
  - imud-mqtt: new `broker_enabled` gates the broker connection/publish;
    keeps a fresh install from repeatedly dialing an unconfigured broker.
  - imud-prometheus: new `http_enabled` gates the /metrics listener.
  - imud-mavlink: unchanged keys (it already had per-transport enables); the
    stock config now ships `enabled = true` like the others.
* Fix: a bridge with `enabled = false` no longer crash-loops under systemd.
  It now signals readiness before exiting cleanly, so the Type=notify unit
  records a successful start and stops (inactive/dead) instead of tripping
  the start-protocol-failure + Restart=on-failure loop. Upgraded systems
  keep their existing /etc/imud/*.conf (dpkg conffiles); bridges left at the
  old `enabled = false` default now exit cleanly rather than looping.


1.6
---
* TCP outputs. Every consumer-facing stream can now serve TCP clients
  alongside (not replacing) its existing transport, because that is how the
  consuming software actually connects — chartplotter apps, signalk-server,
  and ground stations are TCP clients first:
  - [nmea] tcp_enabled: NMEA-over-TCP listener (port 10110) — OpenCPN,
    Navionics, and phone/tablet nav apps connect to tcp://HOST:10110.
  - [stream] tcp_enabled: the loss-free framed binary stream over TCP
    (port 10112) — remote imud-mon, remote bridges, laptop consumers.
  - imud-signalk tcp_enabled: newline-framed deltas on a TCP listener
    (port 10113) for a Signal K server TCP data connection.
  - imud-mavlink tcp_enabled: a TCP listener (port 5760) that
    QGroundControl / Mission Planner connect to directly.
  Each listener accepts up to 8 clients; a slow client drops
  payloads/frames, never stalls the daemon. All tcp_* keys are [restart];
  bind defaults to 0.0.0.0 (set tcp_bind_addr = 127.0.0.1 to stay local).
* BEHAVIOR CHANGE — default output posture. A stock daemon (no config file,
  or one that doesn't say otherwise) now enables ONLY the local AF_UNIX
  [stream] socket: nmea.enabled flipped true -> false, stream.enabled
  flipped false -> true. Bridges and libimud consumers work out of the box,
  and nothing touches the network until an output is explicitly enabled.
  Existing installs that relied on the built-in NMEA-UDP-on default must
  set [nmea] enabled = true (the shipped /etc/imud/imud.conf has carried
  this stream-only posture since 1.5, so installs using it are unaffected).
* libimud: new imud_connect_tcp(host, port) — connect to a daemon's
  [stream] TCP listener; the remote equivalent of imud_connect_stream, same
  framed-read path. Additive (symbol version IMUD_1, SONAME stays
  libimud.so.0). Python: ImudClient.connect_tcp(host, port) /
  ImudClient(tcp=(host, port)), and imud_client.py --tcp HOST[:PORT].
* New companion repository: imud-arduino
  (https://github.com/richcreations/imud-arduino) — an Arduino/ESP32 client
  library (ImudClient) that receives the binary stream over the [stream]
  TCP listener or high-rate UDP, for gauges, displays, and remotes built on
  microcontrollers.
* New shared TCP-server core (src/netserv.c) used by the daemon and both
  bridges: non-blocking accept, broadcast-only (clients are never read),
  the stream thread's proven slow-client policy, single owning thread per
  listener; covered by the new test_netserv suite plus TCP paths in
  test_stream and test_libimud.


1.5.1
-----
* Fix two C11 data races in the 1.5 release (neither is caught by the
  normal or ASan/UBSan test runs):
  - Hot-reload / live navigation values: the fusion and reader threads now
    read the daemon config through a mutex-guarded snapshot, and SIGHUP
    republishes hot fields to each consumer (atomic output rates, a locked
    snapshot for the status thread) instead of mutating a shared struct
    that other threads read concurrently. As a side effect, [fusion]
    heave_tau_s / wave_tau_s / mag_yaw_only now actually take effect on
    SIGHUP (previously they were dropped by the reload).
  - Stream shutdown: the final packet to stream subscribers is now sent by
    the stream thread itself as it exits, so the subscriber list has a
    single owner and is never traversed by the main thread concurrently.
* Reject non-finite and malformed numbers in gpsd / Signal K position input
  (NaN, Inf, truncated/garbage values, out-of-range latitude/longitude) so
  hostile or buggy upstream data can no longer poison declination, the
  magnetic reference, or the centripetal correction.
* Log pthread errors using the function's return code (strerror(rc)) rather
  than errno, which POSIX threads calls do not set.
* New test_concurrency suite drives the reader/fusion/output threads under a
  config-reload storm and stream-shutdown churn; CI runs it under
  ThreadSanitizer.


1.5
----------------
* Capture & replay — the black box: [capture] records every raw sensor
  sample (pre-mount, pre-calibration) to rotating .imucap files; the sim
  driver replays them through the full pipeline (`imud --replay FILE`, or
  [device] sim_file/sim_loop/sim_speed), and imud-status reports the
  recording. Crash-safe, bounded-disk, and documented in docs/capture.md.
  Fixed along the way (found by the new end-to-end replay test): the sim
  driver's pitch axis was mirrored between its accel/mag and gyro outputs,
  the mag reader treated "data not ready" as data (re-fusing a stale
  sample), and two rotations within the same second could reuse a capture
  filename and clobber the earlier file (names are now kept monotonic).
* Per-unit sensor characterization: `imud-cal characterize --from <capture>`
  measures gyro/accel noise density and gyro bias instability (Allan
  variance) into cal.json's "noise" section — a record of what your silicon
  actually does. The numbers are informational: the filter keeps its tuned
  [fusion] mekf_* values, which are held above the raw sensor floor on
  purpose (the gyro terms are the filter's process noise, not a sensor
  readout), so nothing is auto-tuned from them.
* Gyro temperature compensation: `imud-cal fit-temp --from <capture>` fits
  per-axis bias/temperature coefficients; the daemon subtracts the thermal
  term before fusion.
* Fixed: the IMU die temperature was always reported as 25.0 degC. The
  ISM330DHCX and LSM6DSO drivers never enabled FIFO temperature batching, so
  the temperature never updated from its placeholder — in every release to
  date. This affected everything that surfaces it: the binary packet's temp_c,
  imud-mon, the MQTT/InfluxDB/Prometheus telemetry (imud_temperature_celsius
  and friends), and libimud's imud_data_t.temp_c. Temperature now reads real
  values; this is also what makes the new fit-temp mode usable at all, and it
  means the daemon's gyro thermal compensation was previously a no-op.
* Uncalibrated startup (no cal.json): the filter now takes its initial heading
  from the uncalibrated magnetometer — rough, but a real bearing — instead of
  assuming the bow points north. It also no longer counts thousands of phantom
  FIFO overflows (visible in imud-status) while waiting for the first
  magnetometer sample.
* Config values too long for their field are now reported ("value too long —
  truncated") instead of being silently shortened, so an over-long socket path
  can no longer bind somewhere you did not ask for.
* Debian packages: a debian/ build producing ten .debs (imud, imud-utils,
  libimud0, libimud-dev, the five bridges, imud-wmm-data), built for amd64
  and arm64 in CI; version tags now draft a GitHub release with the .debs
  and source tarball attached. imud-mon moved to the new imud-utils package
  (`make install-utils`).
* The shipped reference config now enables only the local AF_UNIX stream
  socket (the input the bridges and libimud consumers read); enable [nmea]
  there for chartplotters. The built-in defaults with no config file are
  unchanged (NMEA on). Fresh installs no longer broadcast UDP out of the box.
* SIGHUP now also refreshes the filter's WMM magnetic-field reference when
  lat/lon or wmm_file change; previously only the declination updated and
  the field-magnitude invariants kept their startup values until restart.
* Experimental-driver fixes (still unvalidated on real silicon): ak09916 no
  longer counts "data not ready"/magnetic overflow as I2C errors, which
  could trigger spurious chip resets after 10 slow reads in a row; the
  icm42688p's hardware timestamps were scaled 25x too coarse and wrapped
  every ~1 s (20-bit counter) — per-driver tick rates and counter
  unwrapping fix the emitted ts_wall_ns/ts_tai_ns for that chip.
* CI: ThreadSanitizer job (all cross-thread stop flags are now C11 _Atomic)
  and libFuzzer harnesses over the four untrusted-input parsers (config,
  gpsd/Signal K JSON, wire packet, capture reader). The packet fuzzer
  immediately found — and this release fixes — a client-side hang:
  imud_true_heading()'s degree normalization looped effectively forever on
  a crafted packet carrying Inf or huge floats, so a hostile UDP datagram
  could wedge any libimud/imud_client.h consumer. Out-of-range values now
  return the -1.0 sentinel.


1.4.1
-----
* World Magnetic Model data split out of the imud package: WMM.COF now
  installs to /usr/share/imud/ via its own target (`make install-wmm-data`,
  packaged as imud-wmm-data, versioned by model epoch — 2025.0) so a new
  five-year model can ship without a daemon release. The [position]
  wmm_file default is now empty = auto-resolve: an operator-supplied
  /etc/imud/WMM.COF (where 1.4-and-earlier installs put it) is preferred,
  then the packaged /usr/share/imud/WMM.COF.


1.4
---
* Sea state: significant wave height (Hs = 4σ(heave)), mean zero-crossing
  wave period, and the vessel's roll and pitch periods and significant
  single amplitudes (2σ), computed from the heave/attitude oscillations
  over a configurable window ([fusion] wave_tau_s, default 120 s). New
  WAVE_VALID flag gates the outputs until the statistics settle.
* Compass health: two always-on diagnostics — mag_anomaly (fractional field-
  magnitude deviation; detects magnetic interference and iron-cal drift) and
  mag_residual (mean absolute heading innovation; calibration health) —
  computed inside the filter, including from rejected measurements.
* New ENGINE_ON flag exports the engine-vibration detector's state; the MQTT
  bridge publishes it as a Home Assistant binary_sensor (engine-hours
  automations).
* Binary stream packet version 14 (260 bytes): the eight fields above are
  appended before the CRC. Wire-pinned consumers must be rebuilt; libimud
  applications keep working without recompiling.
* New bridge: imud-prometheus — serves the fused state as Prometheus
  /metrics gauges (base SI units, flag bits as 0/1 gauges, alerting-ready).
  The first bridge built purely on libimud's ABI-stable interface.
* imud_client.h (the single-header C client) is deprecated: use libimud.
  It remains in the source tree for vendoring and wire-pinned internals but
  is no longer installed by `make install`.
* CI: builds and tests now also run on arm64 (the Pi's architecture) and
  under AddressSanitizer/UBSan; fixed the 1.3 release-verification checks
  (hardening-check fortify false positive on libimud.so, versioned-symbol
  handling in the ABI check).


1.3
---
* New: libimud, an ABI-stable client shared library (libimud.so.0) with a
  public imud.h and pkg-config metadata. Applications link -limud and keep
  working across imud upgrades without recompiling — data is exposed through
  an append-only struct behind an opaque handle, over the local stream socket
  or the UDP stream. See man 3 libimud.
* The four bridges now link libimud (their duplicated stream-socket code is
  gone). No wire-format change: packet version stays 12, and existing
  imud_client.h / imud_client.py consumers are unaffected.


1.2
---
* Binary stream packet version 12 (228 bytes): export more of the fusion state
  imud already computes — estimated gyro bias (x/y/z) and its variance, heave
  rate (vertical velocity), and an accel-quiescence (platform-disturbance)
  metric — plus a new HEAVE_VALID flag that signals when the heave estimate has
  settled. Stream consumers must be rebuilt against the new packet layout.
* Bridges surface the new data where each protocol has a home for it:
  imud-influxdb logs the full v12 diagnostic set (gyro bias + variance, heave
  rate, quiescence, and a heave_valid field); imud-mqtt adds a heave-rate topic;
  and the user-facing bridges (Signal K, MQTT) now withhold heave until the
  estimator settles, so no startup transient is published. imud-mavlink is
  unchanged (ATTITUDE already carries attitude + gyro body rates).


1.1
---
* Optional bridge daemons — each shipped as its own package with its own config
  file, systemd service, man pages, and documentation:
    - imud-signalk   Signal K deltas over UDP
    - imud-mqtt      MQTT topics + Home Assistant discovery (via libmosquitto)
    - imud-influxdb  InfluxDB line protocol over UDP or HTTP (pure C)
    - imud-mavlink   MAVLink v1/v2 (HEARTBEAT/ATTITUDE) over UDP and/or serial
* Binary stream packet version 11: adds heave and magnetic-declination fields.
* Heave estimator and a rough-sea fusion revision.
* Level-gated logging (debug/info/warn/error), journald-aware.
* Documentation brought to GNU + Debian conventions: AUTHORS/NEWS/INSTALL/
  CONTRIBUTING, per-package copyright and changelog, and per-package docs
  installed under /usr/share/doc/.


1.0
---
* Initial release. Quaternion Multiplicative EKF attitude/heading from an I²C
  IMU + magnetometer; NMEA 0183, high-rate binary UDP, and a local AF_UNIX
  subscription stream; World Magnetic Model true heading; gyro / accelerometer /
  magnetometer calibration tools (imud-cal); status and monitoring tools
  (imud-status, imud-mon); a hardened systemd service.
